  "summary": "Protein language models and deep-learning mining are now surfacing enzymes beyond prior annotation, including over 27,000 CAZymes missed by other tools.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is data-driven enzyme and regulator discovery, in which learned models or integrated omics resources are applied to biological sequence and expression space. Deep-learning mining identifies bacterial unspecific peroxygenases (UPOs) expressed in E. coli, challenging the view that this enzyme class is restricted to eukaryotes. CAZyLingua applies protein language models to metagenomic sequences to classify carbohydrate-active enzyme families, recovering over 27,000 CAZymes missed by other tools. The tobacco study integrates field-grown transcriptomic and metabolomic profiles into a 3.17-million-pair regulatory network, nominating NtMYB28 and NtERF167 among regulators of metabolite biosynthesis. AmarylOmicBase supplies the resource layer, unifying assemblies, annotations and expression profiles from 39 studies across 27 Amaryllidoideae species. The items are separate studies rather than a single pipeline; what is new is the use of learned sequence models and network inference for gene and enzyme discovery across microbial and plant systems.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2025-11-30"
}
